<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # What's Your Favorite Food? – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Time to Eat.</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What's Your Favorite Food?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe objects using basic words or phrases, provided they can prepare in advance. Interactive: Interact with questions and answers about a classroom topic.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe food items using basic phrases (e.g., "It's a red apple."). Can ask and answer questions in simple discussions about food with peers (e.g., "What's your favorite food?" "I like ice cream."). Can describe basic food items by following a model provided in advance (e.g., "I like bananas. They are sweet.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and preparing students for the speaking task.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of various food items introduced in previous lessons (e.g., apple, banana, sandwich, water, juice, soup, rice with chicken). Students repeat the names of the food items chorally and individually. The teacher then asks, "What do you see?" and students respond with "I see an apple." or "I see a sandwich." The teacher introduces the question, "Are you hungry?" and students answer, "Yes, I am hungry!" or "No, I am not hungry." The teacher then asks, "What do you want to eat/drink?" and models "I want an apple." or "I want water."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the target phrases: "What's your favorite food?" and "My favorite food is rice with chicken." The teacher also models expressing likes and dislikes: "I like bananas. They are sweet." and "I like apples, but I don't like soup." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /f/ in "favorite" and /s/ in "snack," using exaggerated mouth movements. Teacher also models blending phonemes for "apple" (/ˈæ-p.əl/) and "water" (/ˈw-ɔːt-ər/) by saying the sounds slowly then quickly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of an apple and asks, "Is this a banana?" (Expected: "No.") "Is this an apple?" (Expected: "Yes.") "Do you like apples?" (Expected: "Yes, I do." or "No, I don't.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows two food items (e.g., apple and soup) and asks, "Which one is your favorite?" while pointing to the two items. (Expected: Students point to their favorite.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's Time to Eat."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue between two children talking about their favorite foods and what they want for lunch. The dialogue will feature vocabulary like "apple," "sandwich," "juice," "soup," "tasty," "hungry," "eat," "drink," and phrases such as "What's your favorite food?", "I like...", "Do you like...?", and "I like... but I don't like...".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understands and uses simple spoken language in familiar contexts related to personal information and daily routines.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the dialogue aloud twice, pointing to the characters in the illustrations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> "What are the children talking about?" (Expected: Food / What they like to eat.) "Are they hungry?" (Expected: Yes.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Show a happy/sad face):</b> The teacher makes statements based on the dialogue (e.g., "The boy likes soup." or "The girl likes juice.") and students show a happy face for True and a sad face for False.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> The teacher shows pictures of foods from the dialogue. Students point to the picture as the teacher says, "Show me the sandwich." or "Show me the juice."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking. Applying grammatical structures and vocabulary in controlled and semi-controlled contexts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice asking and answering WH- and yes/no questions about food preferences, expressing likes and dislikes using present simple and compound sentences with 'but'. They will use target vocabulary (apple, banana, sandwich, juice, soup, rice with chicken, tasty, sweet, eat, drink, like, don't like) and focus on pronunciation of /f/ and /s/, and blending of phonemes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Sentence Building & Choral Repetition (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher displays picture cards of food items. Students chorally repeat sentences after the teacher, for example: "I like bananas. They are sweet." "I want a sandwich for lunch." "Do you like soup?" "Yes, I like soup." "No, I don't like soup." The teacher specifically focuses on the pronunciation of /f/ in "favorite" and /s/ in "snack," having students repeat words containing these sounds. The teacher also helps students blend sounds for words like "apple" (/ˈæ-p.əl/) and "water" (/ˈw-ɔːt-ər/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Ask and Answer Chain Drill (Semi-Controlled Practice):</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by asking a student, "What's your favorite food?" or "Do you like apples?" The student answers and then asks the next student in the circle the same or a similar question, continuing the chain. The teacher provides sentence frames on the board for support: "My favorite food is ____." "Yes, I like ____." "No, I don't like ____." The teacher encourages the use of "but" (e.g., "I like apples, but I don't like soup.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Food Preference Interview (Pair Interaction):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a simple worksheet with two columns: "My Partner's Name" and "My Partner's Favorite Food / Likes / Dislikes." Students take turns asking their partner: "What's your favorite food?" and "Do you like...?" They record their partner's answers using short phrases or drawing a happy/sad face next to food pictures on the sheet. The teacher circulates, provides assistance with vocabulary, and corrects pronunciation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's Time to Eat." Focus on fluency and collaborative interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Food Talk Show" – Students will work in groups of three. One student acts as the "Host" and two students are "Guests."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group is given a set of food picture cards (apple, banana, sandwich, juice, soup, rice with chicken, etc.).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "Host" introduces the "guests" and starts the "show" by asking questions about their food preferences. Example questions include: "Hello, what's your name?", "What's your favorite food?", "Do you like [food item]?".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "Guests" answer using the practiced phrases: "My favorite food is [food name]." "Yes, I like [food name]." "No, I don't like [food name]." They are encouraged to add descriptive adjectives (e.g., "It's tasty!") and use "but" (e.g., "I like apples, but I don't like soup.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns playing each role (Host, Guest 1, Guest 2) within their small groups.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing, providing support, and encouraging fluency and natural interaction.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy to gauge student comprehension and application of the target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Food Talk Show" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in their small group "Food Talk Show," asking and answering questions about food preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Students attempt to pronounce words correctly, including /f/ and /s/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Students use at least 4-5 different food vocabulary words and related verbs (like, eat, drink, want).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Fluency:</b> Students speak in simple sentences and are generally understood.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interaction:</b> Students ask and answer questions appropriately in the role-play.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Use:</b> Students demonstrate basic use of "I like...", "Do you like...?", and optionally, "I like... but I don't like...".</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple exit ticket by drawing a happy face if they can ask and answer about favorite foods, and a neutral face if they need more practice. They then complete the sentence: "Today I can say: 'I like ______.'"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and plan for future learning based on lesson outcomes.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new food words did you use today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite part of talking about food with your friends?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to ask and answer basic questions about food preferences, demonstrating comprehension of "What's your favorite food?" and "Do you like...?" The "Food Talk Show" promoted good interaction. Some students still need more practice with the compound sentence structure using "but." The pronunciation of /f/ and /s/ was improving, but blending phonemes needs more explicit attention. For the next lesson, I will provide more scaffolding for sentence construction, especially with dislikes and the conjunction "but," possibly through written sentence frames before speaking.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking practice will be directly applied in Lesson 4 (Writing), where students will write simple sentences and short paragraphs about their favorite foods and what they like/don't like, based on the oral practice from today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>